00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:38.490
On March 16th, on the front page of my Sunday New York Times, which you know I get every week,
在3月16日，在我每周必看的《纽约时报》周日版头版上，

157
00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:41.210
there was an article with this headline.
有一篇文章，标题是这样的。

158
00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:43.590
I'd never been so excited.
我从来没有这么兴奋过。

159
00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:46.190
It said this, quote,
它是这样写的，引述如下，

160
00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:51.750
Landline users remain proudly old-fashioned in the digital age.
在数字时代，座机用户依然自豪地保持着老派作风。

161
00:06:51.750 --> 00:06:55.710
Gosh, this is tying back into our opening of this whole episode.
天哪，这跟我们这期节目的开场呼应上了。

162
00:06:56.390 --> 00:06:58.230
This is what old people talk about.
这就是老年人讨论的话题。

163
00:06:58.470 --> 00:06:59.170
It's true.
千真万确。

164
00:06:59.410 --> 00:07:05.250
I did notice that most of the people quoted in the article were over 60.
我确实注意到，文章中引述的大多数人都超过了60岁。

165
00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:10.750
So, the reason for this article is because there are phone companies that want to phase out the landline.
所以，这篇文章的原因是有些电话公司想要淘汰座机。

166
00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:17.570
The number of copper landlines, which is also known as plain old telephone service, or POTS.
铜线座机的数量，也就是所谓的传统老式电话服务，简称POTS。

167
00:07:18.190 --> 00:07:18.750
POTS?
POTS？

168
00:07:18.850 --> 00:07:19.390
POTS.
POTS。

169
00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:19.850
Okay.
好吧。

170
00:07:20.070 --> 00:07:23.230
If you have POTS, you have plain old telephone service.
如果你有POTS，你就有传统老式电话服务。

171
00:07:23.730 --> 00:07:30.570
The number of people who have this fell by 89% over the last 20 years in California.
在加利福尼亚州，拥有这种服务的人数在过去20年里下降了89%。

172
00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:31.430
Wow.
哇。

173
00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:32.250
I know.
我知道。

174
00:07:32.590 --> 00:07:36.150
So, here's the thing that I want to get out there to everyone.
所以，这是我想对大家说的一件事。

175
00:07:36.450 --> 00:07:37.010
Okay?
好吗？

176
00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:37.520
Okay.
好。

177
00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:38.750
This is important to me.
这对我很重要。

178
00:07:39.070 --> 00:07:39.630
All right.
行吧。

179
00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:41.290
It's like POTS to me.
这对我来说就像POTS一样。

180
00:07:41.730 --> 00:07:43.430
POTS is like POTS to you?
POTS对你来说就像POTS一样？

181
00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:43.890
Yes.
是的。

182
00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:44.390
Okay.
好吧。

183
00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:47.950
A landline is not just a home phone.
座机不仅仅是一部家里的电话。

184
00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:52.290
A true landline has to be hooked up to a copper wire.
真正的座机必须连接到铜线上。

185
00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:55.130
And most phone companies are phasing these out.
而大多数电话公司正在淘汰这些。

186
00:07:55.270 --> 00:07:57.910
And instead, you can have a landline.
取而代之的是，你可以拥有一个座机。

187
00:07:57.910 --> 00:08:01.130
You can have a phone that goes into the wall.
你可以有一个插入墙壁的电话。

188
00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:04.630
But they are putting them on fiber optic cable.
但它们把这些电话连到了光纤电缆上。

189
00:08:04.850 --> 00:08:06.410
And it's not a landline.
而那不是座机。

190
00:08:06.550 --> 00:08:07.930
Not a true landline.
不是真正的座机。

191
00:08:08.050 --> 00:08:15.370
And the reason they're doing this is because those fiber optic cables can also carry cable and internet services.
他们这样做的原因是，这些光纤电缆还可以承载有线电视和互联网服务。

192
00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:20.190
So, it's like they can hook everything up to that as it comes into your house.
所以，就像他们可以把所有东西都连接到进你家的这个上面一样。

193
00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:23.650
And this is what my phone company did to me without telling me.
这就是我的电话公司在没通知我的情况下对我做的事。

194
00:08:24.590 --> 00:08:29.090
They switched my landline, quote unquote, to a fiber optic cable line.
他们把我的“座机”换成了光纤电缆线路。

195
00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:32.070
And I had to call and make them put me back on a copper line.
我不得不打电话让他们把我调回铜线。

196
00:08:33.130 --> 00:08:36.570
Because power failures can take out fiber optic cables.
因为断电会使光纤电缆瘫痪。

197
00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:38.870
They depend on electricity to work.
它们依靠电力才能工作。

198
00:08:39.130 --> 00:08:42.510
So, this is why your Wi-Fi goes out when your power goes out.
所以，这就是为什么停电时你的Wi-Fi会断掉。

199
00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:45.130
But copper wires are self-powered.
但铜线是自供电的。

200
00:08:45.210 --> 00:08:48.390
And they do not go down in the event of a power outage.
而且在停电的情况下，它们不会中断。

201
00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:53.810
I will say, I loved everyone who was quoted in this article.
我得说，我爱极了这篇文章里引用的每一个人。

202
00:08:54.430 --> 00:08:57.210
They are sassy, they are stubborn, and they are my people.
他们尖锐、顽固，而且他们都是我的同道中人。

203
00:08:58.090 --> 00:09:02.290
Derek Shaw, 68, of York, Pennsylvania, loves his landline.
德里克·肖（Derek Shaw），68岁，来自宾夕法尼亚州约克市，他很喜欢他的固定电话。

204
00:09:03.030 --> 00:09:05.590
He also prefers speaking to people face-to-face.
他也更喜欢与人面对面交流。

205
00:09:05.590 --> 00:09:06.790
He's not a fan of Zoom.
他不太喜欢 Zoom。

206
00:09:06.970 --> 00:09:08.390
And he listens to records.
而且他听唱片。

207
00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:09.250
Okay?
好吗？

208
00:09:09.570 --> 00:09:12.910
He said, quote, I've never even thought about giving up my landline.
他说，引述一下，我甚至从未考虑过放弃我的座机。

209
00:09:13.070 --> 00:09:15.050
